/**
 * ============================================================================
 * BOOK MODEL — In-Memory Data Store
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * WHY an in-memory array instead of a database?
 *   - Keeps the example self-contained (no MongoDB/PostgreSQL setup needed).
 *   - Demonstrates CRUD patterns that translate directly to any persistence layer.
 *   - Fast and simple for educational purposes.
 *
 * DATA STRUCTURE CHOICES:
 *   - `books` array: Ordered list of book objects. Arrays are ideal when we need
 *     sequential access, filtering, and sorting — all common in a book catalog.
 *   - `nextId` counter: A simple auto-incrementing ID generator. Using a number
 *     instead of UUIDs keeps the example readable while still demonstrating the
 *     concept of server-side ID generation (vs. client-provided IDs).
 *   - Each book object has: id, title, author, isbn, publishedYear, genre, createdAt, updatedAt.
 *     These fields cover real-world book metadata and give us meaningful filter/sort options.
 */

// ─── In-Memory Storage ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

/**
 * Array holding all book records.
 * Chosen over a Map/Object because:
 *   1. Arrays preserve insertion order (useful for list endpoints).
 *   2. Filter/sort/map operations are idiomatic on arrays.
 *   3. JSON serialization is straightforward (just JSON.stringify(books)).
 */
const books = [];

/**
 * Auto-incrementing ID counter.
 * Starts at 1 so IDs are human-friendly (not zero-based).
 * In a real app, you'd use a database sequence or UUID generator.
 */
let nextId = 1;

// ─── Seed Data ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

/**
 * Pre-populate with sample books so the API isn't empty on first launch.
 * This gives immediate feedback when testing and demonstrates realistic data shapes.
 */
