## 4. File: `middleware/errorHandler.js` — Centralised Error Handling

```js
/**
 * middleware/errorHandler.js
 *
 * Provides a custom AppError class and an Express middleware that formats
 * errors into consistent JSON responses.
 *
 * Why a custom error class?
 * - Distinguishes client‑side errors (4xx) from server‑side failures (5xx).
 * - Carries both a human‑readable message and an HTTP status code.
 * - Allows route handlers to throw `new AppError('message', 400)` instead of
 *   manually calling `res.status(…).json(…)`.
 */

/**
 * Custom error class extending the built‑in Error.
 * Adds a `statusCode` property so downstream middleware can set the HTTP response code.
 */
class AppError extends Error {
  /**
   * @param {string} message - Human‑readable description of the error.
   * @param {number} statusCode - HTTP status code (e.g., 400, 404, 500).
   */
  constructor(message, statusCode) {
    super(message);
    this.statusCode = statusCode;

    // Capture a stack trace for debugging (optional but helpful in development)
    Error.captureStackTrace(this, this.constructor);
  }
}

/**
 * Express error‑handling middleware.
 * Signature must be `(err, req, res, next)` — Express recognises this as an error handler.
 *
 * @param {AppError} err - The error object thrown by a route or middleware.
 * @param {import('express').Request} req - Express request object (unused here).
 * @param {import('express').Response} res - Express response object.
 * @param {Function} next - Next middleware function (not called in error handler).
 */
function errorHandler(err, _req, res, _next) {
  // Default to 500 if no statusCode was provided (defensive programming)
  const statusCode = err.statusCode || 500;

  // In production you might hide the message for security; here we always return it for learning.
  res.status(statusCode).json({
    error: err.message,
    statusCode,
  });
}

module.exports = { AppError, errorHandler };
```

**Why this pattern?**  
- Throwing `new AppError('Not found', 404)` from a route lets Express skip all remaining middleware and jump straight to the error handler.  
- The middleware centralises response formatting, so every error looks the same (`{ error, statusCode }`).  
- `Error.captureStackTrace` preserves the call stack for debugging without affecting production performance significantly.

